They also have a simple Chow, so maybe not all honors. But they could be collecting winds. Their history shows they Peng F1 and Peng F3, and Chi B5. So they are active. I should avoid discarding winds or dragons that might give them Pung or Kong. But my hand has no winds or dragons except the Kong of W1 which is not wind. So discarding B7, B1, etc. are safe from winds.\n\nBut also previous player has T9 Pung, so T9 is dangerous.\n\nI should think about tile efficiency: which discard leaves me with the best potential to form melds quickly.\n\nOption 1: Discard T2. Then hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, B1, B7, B8, B9, B9. That's 10 tiles? Wait, I have 11 now, discard one leaves 10, but I need to account for the Chow: if I keep B7,B8,B9 Chow, then after discard T2, hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, B1, B9 (since B7,B8,B9 used), that's 7 tiles? Let's list properly:\n\nCurrent hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B7, B8, B9, B9. (11 tiles)\n\nIf I discard T2, hand becomes: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, B1, B7, B8, B9, B9. (10 tiles)\n\nNow, I can consider the Chow B7,B8,B9 as one meld, then remaining: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, B1, B9 (since one B9 used). That's 7 tiles: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, B1, B9. I need two melds and a pair from these 7. Possible: pair W4 or W7, then try to form melds from W2, W4/W7, B1, B9. Not great.\n\nOption 2: Discard B1. Then hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B7, B8, B9, B9. (10 tiles). Keep Chow B7,B8,B9: then remaining: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B9. (7 tiles). Similar.\n\nOption 3: Discard W2. Then hand: W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B7, B8, B9, B9. Keep Chow B7,B8,B9: remaining: W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B9. (7 tiles). Still similar.\n\nOption 4: Discard B7. Then hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B8, B9, B9. (10 tiles). Now I don't have the Chow. But I have B8 and B9,B9. Could form Chow with B7,B8,B9 but no B7. Or Pung with B9. So maybe discard B7 breaks the Chow, but maybe it's not a strong meld because it uses terminals. Alternatively, I could aim for a pair of B9 and try for Pung, and use B8 with other tiles? B8 could combine with B7,B9 or B6,B7, but B7 is gone. So discard B7 might be okay if I focus on other melds.\n\nOption 5: Discard B8. Similar to discarding B7, breaks the Chow.\n\nOption 6: Discard W4. Then hand: W2, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B7, B8, B9, B9. (10 tiles). I have a pair of W7 and B9. Keep Chow B7,B8,B9: remaining: W2, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B9. (7 tiles). Now I have only one W4, so less flexibility.\n\nOption 7: Discard W7. Then hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, T2, B1, B7, B8, B9, B9. (10 tiles). Keep Chow B7,B8,B9: remaining: W2, W4, W4, W7, T2, B1, B9. (7 tiles). Still.\n\nOption 8: Discard B9. Then hand: W2, W4, W4, W7, W7, T2, B1, B7, B8, B9. (10 tiles). Now I have a single B9, and B7,B8 could form Chow with B6 or B9. But no pair of B9.\n\nFrom these, discarding T2 or B1 seems to remove an isolated terminal/simple that doesn't connect well. T2 has some connection to T1,T3, but T1 and T3 are in a Chow by across player, so less available. B1 is terminal and only useful for Pung, which is hard. So discarding B1 might be good because it's a terminal and reduces the number of terminals, though I already have W1 Kong terminal.\n\nAlso, I should consider the points. I have a Kong, which gives points for Kong itself? Actually, Kong is not a scoring pattern by itself; it contributes to patterns like Four Kongs, Three Kongs, etc. I have one concealed Kong, which gives 4 points for Concealed Kong? Wait, patterns: Concealed Kong is worth 4 points? No, according to the list: Concealed Kong is worth 2 points? Let's check: \"Concealed Kong: A hand with a concealed kong.\" worth 2 points. Yes, so I already have 2 points from that. Also, maybe All Pungs if I get all pungs? But I have a Chow potential.\n\nTo win, I need at least 8 points. So I need additional patterns. With Concealed Kong (2), and if I get All Simples (2) but I have terminals, so not. Maybe Dragon Pung? I have no dragons. Prevalent Wind? East Wind is prevailing, but I have no East Wind. Seat Wind?